  This paper provides an overview of new tools for \LaTeX\ package
  authors that I created during the development of \textsf{minted}
  [7] version 3.
  
  The fvextra [8]
  package can now save the contents of an environment verbatim in
  memory, in a form that allows line-by-line iteration.  Then the
  saved contents or a modified version can later be typeset or
  written to an external file.

  The new \textsf{latex2pydata}
  \LaTeX\ package [9] allows key-value data to be
  written to a file in Python literal format [11].
  Alternatively, data can be accumulated in an in-memory buffer which
  allows hashing or modification before optionally being written to
  a file.  The new \textsf{latex2pydata} Python
  package~[6] provides data loading and processing
  capabilities for data saved by the \textsf{latex2pydata}
  \LaTeX\ package.  Together, these packages make it possible to pass
  structured data from \LaTeX\ into Python.
  
  The new
  \textsf{latexrestricted}~[5] Python package
  provides access to the file system and subprocesses in a manner
  consistent with \LaTeX\ security settings.  This simplifies the
  process of creating Python executables that meet the security
  requirements for \LaTeX\ restricted shell escape.
